{"name":"Saturn","hex":"#fae5bf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Saturn","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The sixth planet of the solar system, known for its large rings, and until recent times the furthest known; represented in astronomy and astrology by ♄.","The god of fertility and agriculture, equivalent to the Greek Cronos."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Sable (black), in the postmedieval practice of blazoning the tinctures in the arms of certain sovereign people and places as planets.","Lead (the metal)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Saturn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saturn","blocks":[{"text":"Saturn is the sixth planet from the Sun, and is the second largest planet in the Solar System, after Jupiter. It is a gas giant, with an average radius of about 9 times that of Earth. It has an eighth of the average density of Earth, but is over 95 times more massive. Even though Saturn is almost as big as Jupiter, it has less than a third of its mass. Saturn orbits the Sun at a distance of 9.59 AU (1,434 million km), with an orbital period of 29.45 years."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Saturn_global_view_from_Cassini%2C_rings_open_Better_Colour.png/330px-Saturn_global_view_from_Cassini%2C_rings_open_Better_Colour.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturn"},{"title":"Saturn (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"Saturn was a god in ancient Roman religion, and a character in Roman mythology. He was described as a god of time, generation, dissolution, abundance, wealth, agriculture, periodic renewal and liberation. Saturn's mythological reign was depicted as a Golden Age of abundance and peace. After the Roman conquest of Greece, he was conflated with the Greek Titan Cronus. Saturn's consort was his sister Ops, with whom he fathered Jupiter, Neptune, Pluto, Juno, Ceres and Vesta."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Saturn_with_head_protected_by_winter_cloak%2C_holding_a_scythe_in_his_right_hand%2C_fresco_from_the_House_of_the_Dioscuri_at_Pompeii%2C_Naples_Archaeological_Museum_%2823497733210%29.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturn_(mythology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Saturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png/500px-Saturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Saturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png/1280px-Saturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Saturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png/500px-Saturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The size of Saturn, compared to Earth and its moon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaturn_compared_to_Earth_and_the_Moon.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Saturn_Storm.jpg/500px-Saturn_Storm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Saturn_Storm.jpg/500px-Saturn_Storm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A global storm girdles the planet in 2011. The storm passes around the planet, such that the storm's head (bright area) passes its tail.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaturn_Storm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Saturn_-_July_24_2013_%2848594309686%29.jpg/500px-Saturn_-_July_24_2013_%2848594309686%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_cont